<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Test stack. xunit v3 rather than v2: it is what the sibling repositories standardised on,
    and a v3 test project is a self-executing Exe (see tests/Directory.Build.props).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.8.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.5" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Microsoft's FakeTimeProvider, for tests only. Debouncing is the one thing in this repository
    that genuinely depends on the passage of time, and the contract forbids Task.Delay as a
    substitute for coordination, so the clock has to be something a test advances by hand.

    Taken as a dependency rather than hand-written, unlike the deliberate refusal of a NuGet client
    in ADR 0012, and the difference is which side of the boundary it lands on: nothing ships with
    this, and the alternative is a fake timer implementation whose Change semantics would themselves
    need testing.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing" Version="10.8.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    MSBuild, for the provider package only. The core references none of this and an architecture
    test enforces that.

    Every one of these except the locator carries ExcludeAssets="runtime" at the reference site, and
    that is not optional: MSBuild's assemblies must come from the SDK the locator finds at run time,
    not from these packages. Shipping both copies is the classic way to get a process that loads two
    different MSBuilds and fails somewhere unrelated. The locator itself is exempt because it is what
    does the finding, so it has to load first and on its own.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Build.Locator" Version="1.11.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Build" Version="18.8.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Build.Framework" Version="18.8.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Reads .sln and .slnx, and is the library the SDK itself uses for both. MSBuild vendors a copy
    internally, so it cannot be reached through Microsoft.Build; this is the public one.

    It is here rather than MSBuild's own SolutionFile because SolutionFile loses solution folders
    entirely for .slnx: projects come back carrying a parent folder guid while the folder appears in
    neither ProjectsInOrder nor ProjectsByGuid, so the reference resolves to nothing. Measured, not
    assumed. This one returns identical, complete results for both formats.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.VisualStudio.SolutionPersistence" Version="1.0.52" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    For the package-management package only, which is the one allowed to host NuGet.

    Just the versioning library, and deliberately not NuGet.Protocol. Comparing versions is where
    being subtly wrong is silent and harmful: SemVer 2 ordering has a fourth numeric field, a
    prerelease that sorts *before* the release it precedes, dot-separated identifiers that compare
    numerically or lexically depending on their shape, and build metadata that is ignored entirely.
    Nobody should reimplement that. This assembly has no dependencies of its own, so the cost is one
    file.

    Reading a search response, by contrast, is a documented JSON shape and is read by hand for the
    same reason project.assets.json is (ADR 0012).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="NuGet.Versioning" Version="7.6.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
